### TITLE ###
AccessorsTypeTheirResult
### DESCRIPTION ###
Each accessor decides the type of the whole invocation: the two output
accessors are strings, `.code` is an int and `.ok` is a bool. These are the
same types the matching capture targets get, because both come from
rl.shellStreamType.
### INPUT ###
out = $`cmd`.stdout
err = $`cmd`.stderr
code = $`cmd`.code
ok = $`cmd`.ok
### CHECK ###
# Identifier types
  out @ 1:1 -> <no-type>
  err @ 2:1 -> <no-type>
  code @ 3:1 -> <no-type>
  ok @ 4:1 -> <no-type>

# Symbol types
  code (local): int
  err (local): str
  ok (local): bool
  out (local): str

# Diagnostics
  (none)

### TITLE ###
BareInvocationInExpressionPositionIsRejected
### DESCRIPTION ###
A command has no value on its own, so using one where a value is wanted is an
error rather than a guess. Shell's ambiguity between "succeeded" and "printed
something" is exactly what naming the accessor removes.
### INPUT ###
if $`cmd`:
    pass
### CHECK ###
# Identifier types
  (none)

# Symbol types
  (none)

# Diagnostics
  [error] RAD40025 @ 1:4 - A shell command has no value on its own. Say which result you want: `.stdout`, `.stderr`, `.code`, or `.ok`
    help: `.stdout` and `.stderr` give you the output and fail if the command did. `.ok` (bool) and `.code` (int) describe the outcome and never fail, so use those to test a command rather than read it.

### TITLE ###
ComputedCommandGetsTheMigrationFix
### DESCRIPTION ###
`$cmds[1]` ran what `cmds[1]` held before v0.12. It still parses, so the
checker can say what it used to mean and hand over the parenthesized spelling
rather than failing as a syntax error that could do neither.
### INPUT ###
cmds = ["echo a", "echo b"]
$cmds[1]
### CHECK ###
# Identifier types
  cmds @ 1:1 -> <no-type>
  cmds @ 2:2 -> str[]

# Symbol types
  cmds (local): str[]

# Diagnostics
  [error] RAD40026 @ 2:1 - `$` now takes only the command, so an index here reads the command's result rather than building it. This used to index the value first and run the result.
    help: Wrap the command in parentheses: `$(cmds[1])`. See https://amterp.dev/rad/migrations/v0.12/

### TITLE ###
MethodOnCommandIsRejected
### DESCRIPTION ###
This ran ECHO HI before v0.12 - the method transformed the command text rather
than its output, and nothing reported it.
### INPUT ###
x = $`echo hi`.upper()
### CHECK ###
# Identifier types
  x @ 1:1 -> <no-type>

# Symbol types
  x (local): dynamic

# Diagnostics
  [error] RAD40026 @ 1:16 - A method here would have been applied to the command text, not its output - read a result first, then transform it.
    help: Insert an accessor: `$`cmd`.stdout.upper()` rather than `$`cmd`.upper()`.

### TITLE ###
UnknownAccessorGetsDidYouMean
### DESCRIPTION ###
There are four results and no others, so a near-miss name is worth correcting
rather than just rejecting.
### INPUT ###
x = $`cmd`.output
### CHECK ###
# Identifier types
  x @ 1:1 -> <no-type>

# Symbol types
  x (local): dynamic

# Diagnostics
  [error] RAD40026 @ 1:12 - 'output' is not a shell result. Use one of: `.stdout`, `.stderr`, `.code`, or `.ok`
    help: did you mean 'stdout'?

### TITLE ###
InterpolationQuotingIsCheckedInlineToo
### DESCRIPTION ###
RAD40023 has to see both invocation forms. Checking only the statement form
would silently cover half the language.
### INPUT ###
msg = "a b"
x = $`git commit -m "{msg}"`.stdout
### CHECK ###
# Identifier types
  msg @ 1:1 -> <no-type>
  x @ 2:1 -> <no-type>
  msg @ 2:23 -> <no-type>

# Symbol types
  msg (local): str
  x (local): str

# Diagnostics
  [error] RAD40023 @ 2:22 - Quotes around this interpolation end up in the argument itself
    help: Remove them - Rad quotes shell interpolations for you. For literal text plus a value, build the string first and interpolate that.
